.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "why", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 1. Why Query Objects
  :markdown
    Reads accumulate in two bad places. **Controllers** grow
    `where` chains that depend on `params`, and every action that lists the
    same thing reinvents them slightly differently. **Models** grow scopes
    until the model file is mostly about how other people search it, and
    scopes can't say which arguments they need or what a bad one looks
    like.

    A query object fixes both. The controller asks one named question —
    `OrderQuery.overdue(days: 30)` — and the answer is a relation it can
    paginate, count, or hand to a view. The model keeps its scopes as
    vocabulary. And the query class is the one place to read, test, and
    change how that question is answered. The pairing with interactions is
    deliberate: **interactions mutate, queries read**, and neither has to
    know how the other works.

.mt-6
  %div{ class: "grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 lg:grid-cols-2" }
    %div
      = daisy_badge("Before", css: "badge-ghost mb-2")
      = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
        :plain
          def index
            @orders = Order.where(status: params[:status])
            @orders = @orders.where("total > ?", params[:min_total]) if params[:min_total].present?
            @orders = @orders.where(customer_id: current_account.customer_ids)
            @orders = @orders.order(placed_at: :desc)
          end
    %div
      = daisy_badge("After", css: "badge-primary mb-2")
      = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
        :plain
          def index
            @orders = OrderQuery.for_account(
              account_id: current_account.id,
              status: params[:status],
              min_total: params[:min_total]
            ).order(placed_at: :desc)
          end

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "install", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 2. Installation
  :markdown
    Add the gem — note the hyphen; `active_query` with an underscore is an
    unrelated, abandoned gem — and run `bundle install`. It needs Ruby 3.2+
    and ActiveRecord 6.1 through 8.x; there is nothing to generate or
    configure.

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # Gemfile
      gem "active-query", "~> 0.2"

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    Mirror the interactions layout: a base module in `app/queries/` that
    every query object includes, so shared behaviour has a home later.
    ActiveQuery registers classes that include it through an intermediate
    concern just as well as direct includes:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # app/queries/application_query.rb
      module ApplicationQuery
        extend ActiveSupport::Concern
        include ActiveQuery::Base
      end

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "define", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 3. Defining a Query Object
  :markdown
    A query object names its model and declares queries with `query`: a
    name, a one-line **description**, optionally an argument definition,
    and a lambda that builds on `scope` — the model's `all` relation, with
    ActiveQuery's extra operations mixed in. Each query becomes a class
    method returning a plain `ActiveRecord::Relation`:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # app/queries/order_query.rb
      class OrderQuery
        include ApplicationQuery

        model_name "Order"

        query :overdue, "Orders past their due date that are still unpaid",
          { days: { type: Integer, default: 0 } },
          ->(days:) { scope.where(paid_at: nil).lt(:due_on, days.days.ago.to_date) }

        query :for_account, "An account's orders, optionally filtered by status and minimum total",
          {
            account_id: { type: Integer },
            status:     { type: String, optional: true },
            min_total:  { type: Float, optional: true }
          },
          ->(account_id:, status:, min_total:) {
            scope.where(account_id: account_id)
                 .if(status.present?, -> { where(status: status) })
                 .if(min_total.present?, -> { gt(:total, min_total) })
          }
      end

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      OrderQuery.overdue                          # => ActiveRecord::Relation
      OrderQuery.overdue(days: 30).count
      OrderQuery.for_account(account_id: 7, status: "shipped").includes(:customer).limit(20)

= doc_note(modifier: :warning, title: "Always Set model_name", css: "mt-6") do
  :markdown
    ActiveQuery infers the model only from a **namespaced** class named
    `Query` — `Orders::Query` resolves to `Order`. The flat `OrderQuery`
    form does *not* infer (the suffix isn't stripped), and the first query
    you call raises "Model not defined". Declaring `model_name "Order"`
    costs one line and works for both spellings, so make it a habit.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "arguments", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 4. Arguments
  :markdown
    The argument definition is the part a scope can't give you. Each
    argument has a **type** (`String`, `Integer`, `Float`, `Boolean`, or a
    type class you register), and may be `optional: true` *or* carry a
    `default:` — never both. Calls are validated before the lambda runs,
    and the errors say exactly what went wrong:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      OrderQuery.for_account                          # ArgumentError: Params missing: [:account_id]
      OrderQuery.for_account(account_id: 7, foo: 1)   # ArgumentError: Unknown params: [:foo]
      OrderQuery.for_account(account_id: "seven")     # ArgumentError: :account_id must be of type Integer

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    Values are **coerced** before they are validated: `"7"` becomes `7`,
    `"true"` becomes `true`, a symbol becomes a string. That is what lets a
    query object take `params` values directly without a cast at every
    call site. Optional arguments arrive as `nil`, so the lambda's `if`
    guards are how "not given" becomes "not filtered". If you need a type
    the gem doesn't know — a UUID, a currency code — subclass
    `ActiveQuery::Types::Base` and register it once in an initializer.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "compose", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 5. Composing Queries
  :markdown
    Because every query returns a relation, composition is just chaining:
    `OrderQuery.overdue.merge(OrderQuery.for_account(account_id: 7))`, or
    call one query object's result from another's lambda. Inside the
    lambdas, three tools keep the code flat:

    - **`if` / `unless`** apply a block of conditions only when a test is
      true — the idiomatic way to handle optional filters.
    - **Comparison and pattern operations** — `gt`, `gteq`, `lt`, `lteq`,
      `like`, `start_like`, `end_like` — replace the string SQL fragments
      that `where` needs for anything but equality.
    - **A `Scopes` module** inside the query object defines named,
      reusable pieces available on `scope`, so a long query reads as a
      sentence.

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      class OrderQuery
        include ApplicationQuery
        model_name "Order"

        module Scopes
          include ActiveQuery::Scopes

          scope :unpaid, -> { where(paid_at: nil) }
          scope :placed_since, ->(date:) { gteq(:placed_at, date) }
        end

        query :recent_unpaid, "Unpaid orders placed in the last month",
          -> { scope.unpaid.placed_since(date: 1.month.ago) }

        query :search, "Orders whose reference starts with a prefix",
          { prefix: { type: String } },
          ->(prefix:) { scope.start_like(:reference, prefix) }
      end

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    When a query outgrows a lambda — joins, grouping, a `having` clause —
    move it to a **resolver**, a class inheriting from
    `ActiveQuery::Resolver` whose `resolve` method receives the same
    keyword arguments and builds on `scope`. The query object stays a
    readable index of *what* can be asked; the resolver holds *how*.

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # app/queries/resolvers/top_customers_resolver.rb
      class TopCustomersResolver < ActiveQuery::Resolver
        def resolve(min_orders: 5)
          scope.joins(:orders)
               .group("customers.id")
               .having("COUNT(orders.id) >= ?", min_orders)
               .select("customers.*, COUNT(orders.id) AS order_count")
        end
      end

      class CustomerQuery
        include ApplicationQuery
        model_name "Customer"

        query :top, "Customers with at least min_orders orders",
          { min_orders: { type: Integer } },
          resolver: TopCustomersResolver
      end

= doc_note(modifier: :tip, title: "Every Query Is Self-Describing", css: "mt-6") do
  :markdown
    `OrderQuery.queries` returns each query's name, description, and
    argument definition, and `ActiveQuery::Base.registry` lists every
    query object in the app. That is enough to generate an admin
    "reports" page, a CLI, or documentation from the query layer itself —
    and it is why the description argument is required, not decorative.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "testing", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 6. Testing a Query Object
  :markdown
    A query object is tested against a real database with a handful of
    records — this is what
    [factories](#{guide_path("test_factories")}) are for. Create the rows
    that should match and one that shouldn't, call the query, and assert
    on the result. Argument validation gets one example per rule, and a
    `to_sql` assertion is worth it only when the SQL shape *is* the
    behaviour (an index you depend on, a join you must avoid):

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # spec/queries/order_query_spec.rb
      RSpec.describe OrderQuery do
        describe ".overdue" do
          it "returns unpaid orders past their due date" do
            overdue = create(:order, :unpaid, due_on: 10.days.ago)
            create(:order, :unpaid, due_on: 2.days.from_now)   # not yet due
            create(:order, :paid,   due_on: 10.days.ago)       # paid, so not overdue

            expect(described_class.overdue).to contain_exactly(overdue)
          end

          it "widens the window with days:" do
            create(:order, :unpaid, due_on: 10.days.ago)

            expect(described_class.overdue(days: 30)).to be_empty
          end
        end

        describe ".for_account" do
          it "requires account_id" do
            expect { described_class.for_account }.to raise_error(ArgumentError, /account_id/)
          end
        end
      end

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "when", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 7. Scope, Query Object, or Interaction?
  :markdown
    Three tools that all "get data" is a recipe for arguments unless the
    boundaries are stated. Ours:

